# AI Provider Implementation

Applies to anything under `src/vs/ai/provider/` — new providers, provider registry changes, and provider error handling.

## The `IAIProvider` contract

Every provider must implement `IAIProvider` with these required fields and methods:

```typescript
export class MyProvider implements IAIProvider {
    readonly id = 'myprovider';
    readonly name = 'My Provider';
    readonly capabilities: ProviderCapabilities = {
        supportsStreaming: true,
        supportsSystemMessages: true,
        supportsFunctionCalling: false,
        maxContextLength: 128000,
        supportedModes: ['coding', 'architect', 'debug', 'learning']
    };

    async initialize(config: ProviderConfig): Promise<void> { }
    async sendRequest(context: AIRequestContext): Promise<AIResponse> { }
    async *streamRequest(context: AIRequestContext): AsyncIterable<AIResponseChunk> { }
}
```

- `capabilities` must accurately declare `supportsStreaming`, `supportsSystemMessages`, `supportsFunctionCalling`, `maxContextLength`, and `supportedModes` — the rest of the AI layer (mode gating, streaming UI, function-calling dispatch) branches on these, so an inaccurate flag causes silent feature breakage rather than a type error.
- `initialize` must not throw for missing optional config; validate required config and fail with an `AIProviderError`, not a bare `Error`.

## Registration

Register the provider instance in `ProviderRegistry` — don't let callers `new` a provider directly:

```typescript
// In providerRegistry.ts
registry.register('myprovider', new MyProvider());
```

## Configuration schema contribution

Every provider needs its own namespaced settings under `ai.provider.<name>.*` added in `configuration.ts`:

```typescript
// In configuration.ts
'ai.provider.myprovider.apiKey': {
    type: 'string',
    default: '',
    description: 'My Provider API Key'
}
```

Never log or persist the raw API key value outside this settings storage path — see the privacy rules in `30-context-and-token-budgets.mdc`.

## Error hierarchy

Providers throw a typed error, not a bare `Error`, so callers can branch on retryability:

```typescript
export class AIProviderError extends Error {
    constructor(
        message: string,
        public readonly provider: string,
        public readonly code: string,
        public readonly retryable: boolean = false
    ) {
        super(message);
    }
}

export class AIRateLimitError extends AIProviderError {
    constructor(provider: string, public readonly retryAfter: number) {
        super('Rate limit exceeded', provider, 'RATE_LIMIT', true);
    }
}
```

Any new provider-specific error subclasses `AIProviderError` and sets `retryable` correctly rather than introducing a parallel error shape.

## Retry-with-backoff usage pattern

Callers (and the provider's own internal retry wrapper, where applicable) should retry only on `retryable` errors, honoring `retryAfter`:

```typescript
async function sendWithRetry(context: AIRequestContext): Promise<AIResponse> {
    try {
        return await provider.sendRequest(context);
    } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof AIRateLimitError && error.retryable) {
            await delay(error.retryAfter * 1000);
            return sendWithRetry(context);
        }
        throw error;
    }
}
```

- Always have a fallback/exit path (max retry count or a fallback provider) — never retry unconditionally on every `AIProviderError`, only on ones explicitly marked `retryable`.
- Non-retryable errors (auth failure, invalid request) must propagate immediately with a meaningful message — don't swallow them into a generic failure.
